# models/file

The metadata index of a blog's folder: a record for every file and every directory in it, so the S3 backend of `storage/folder` can stat, list, walk and resolve case without asking S3. It is a sibling of `models/entry`, and a file and its entry join on the same path string. See [#2258](https://github.com/blotcms/blot/issues/2258) ("Metadata index (Redis): `models/file`").

- `storage/folder`'s S3 backend (`app/storage/folder/s3.js`) is its only writer. Nothing else calls the write functions. The local-disk backend, which every blog still uses, does not touch it.
- It is derived from S3: the backend writes to S3 first and to the index after, so a crash leaves the index behind S3, never ahead. It can be rebuilt from a full listing at any time (`rebuild`, and `scripts/storage/rebuild-file-index.js`).
- Writes read what they need, then apply everything in one `multi()`/`exec()`. That is only safe because each blog has one writer at a time, the sync lock (`app/sync/lock.js`). No Lua and no `WATCH`.

---

## Records

`{ type: "file" | "dir", size, modified, sha1 }`: size in bytes, modified in milliseconds since the epoch, sha1 the SHA-1 of the file's bytes in hex. A directory has size 0 and no hash. `sha1` is `""` when it isn't known, which only a rebuild without hashing produces.

Paths are normalised with `helper/pathNormalizer`, exactly as entry keys are (`models/entry/key.js`): a leading slash, no trailing slash, no double slashes, NFC. The folder itself is `/`, always exists, and has no record of its own (`get` returns a directory with `modified` 0).

---

## Redis keys

No key per file. There is a hash, a lookup and a names set per directory, and two keys per blog. Every key starts `blog:{blogID}:file`, so blog deletion's `blog:{blogID}:*` sweep takes it with the rest.

| Key | Type | Contents |
|-----|------|----------|
| `blog:{blogID}:file:dir:{dirPath}` | hash | child name → packed record `type\|size\|modified\|sha1` |
| `blog:{blogID}:file:lower:{dirPath}` | hash | lowercased child name → the child names that fold to it, joined by `/` and sorted |
| `blog:{blogID}:file:names:{dirPath}` | sorted set | every child name (as `key.member` spells it), all at score 0, so a page of the directory in name order is one `ZRANGEBYLEX` |
| `blog:{blogID}:file:paths` | sorted set | every path below the root, all at score 0, for `ZRANGEBYLEX` subtree queries |
| `blog:{blogID}:file:totals` | hash | `bytes`, `files`: the folder's totals, for the storage quota |

For a folder holding `/Posts/a.txt` (5 bytes), `/posts/b.txt` (3 bytes) and an empty directory `/drafts`:

```
blog:X:file:dir:/          Posts  -> d|0|1700000000000|
                           posts  -> d|0|1700000000000|
                           drafts -> d|0|1700000000000|
blog:X:file:lower:/        posts  -> Posts/posts
                           drafts -> drafts
blog:X:file:names:/        Posts drafts posts
blog:X:file:dir:/Posts     a.txt  -> f|5|1700000000000|aaf4c61d...
blog:X:file:lower:/Posts   a.txt  -> a.txt
blog:X:file:names:/Posts   a.txt
blog:X:file:dir:/posts     b.txt  -> f|3|1700000000000|e9d71f5e...
blog:X:file:lower:/posts   b.txt  -> b.txt
blog:X:file:names:/posts   b.txt
blog:X:file:paths          /Posts /Posts/a.txt /drafts /posts /posts/b.txt
blog:X:file:totals         bytes -> 8, files -> 2
```

The packing is the record type's first letter (`f` or `d`), then size, modified and sha1, separated by `|`. A name may contain `|`, but it is only ever a field, never part of a value. No name can contain `/`, so it separates the names in the lowercased lookup. An empty directory has no hash of its own; its record in its parent's hash is what makes it exist.

Case-insensitive resolution looks each segment up in its directory's lowercased lookup. Names which differ only by case (`/Posts` and `/posts`) share one field, so neither hides the other: the exact name wins, otherwise the first in sorted order, by `helper/caseSensitivePath.pick` over the stored names. Each segment is resolved on its own, without going back to try a sibling, as on disk.

Subtree queries use the bounds `[/dir/` and `(/dir0` (`0` is the character after `/`), so `/dir b` and `/dir0` stay out.

### Paging a directory

Listings sort by name in UTF-16 code-unit order, as JavaScript compares strings and the local backend sorts, so `storage/folder`'s cursor is just the last name of a page. `list` reads a page with `ZRANGEBYLEX` on the names set, from just after that name, one more than the limit to tell whether another page follows, then `HMGET`s the page's records from the hash: a page costs the same however large the directory. A cursor need not name an entry still there.

`ZRANGEBYLEX` compares UTF-8 bytes, which is code point order. That differs from code-unit order only for characters from U+E000 to U+FFFF against characters beyond U+FFFF, so a member spells those with U+10FFFF in front (and U+10FFFF itself as U+10FFFF, NUL), which puts them in code-unit order (`key.member`, `key.nameOf`). Every other name is its own member.

Paging through the path set instead needs no new key, but a directory's children are interleaved there with all their descendants, so skipping each subdirectory's subtree costs a round trip per subdirectory on the page.

Names sets came after the rest of the index, so a directory may lack one. `list` then reads its hash whole and sorts it, as every listing did before. Only a writer, under the sync lock, makes a names set: when `setMany` adds a name to a directory without one, it builds the whole set from the hash in the same exec, so a names set that exists is always complete. A remove never creates one (`ZREM` of a missing key does nothing). `addMissingNames(blogID, { apply })` builds the rest, from `scripts/storage/add-file-index-names.js`, which holds each blog's sync lock while it does.

Memory: a names set holds each name once more, beside its field in the hash and its full path in the path set. A small directory's set is a ziplist, a few bytes per name over the names themselves, plus the key; above `zset-max-ziplist-entries` (128 by default, Redis 6.2) or with a name over 64 bytes it is a skiplist, roughly 90 to 100 bytes per name on top of the name. Against an index of about 250 bytes per path, that is roughly a tenth to a fifth more for most folders.

---

## Functions

All return promises.

| Function | |
|----------|---|
| `get(blogID, path)` | The record, or null |
| `list(blogID, dir, { after, limit })` | One page of a directory sorted by name (code-unit order), starting after the name `after`: `{ entries: [{ name, ...record }], next }`, next the `after` of the following page, or null after the last. `limit` defaults to 1000. Null if dir is not a directory |
| `listAll(blogID, dir)` | Every entry of a directory, sorted by name, or null. Pages through `list`, but holds the whole directory |
| `subtree(blogID, dir, { pageSize })` | Async iterator over every path below dir, in byte order of the whole path |
| `resolveCaseInsensitive(blogID, path)` | The stored spelling of path, or null |
| `totals(blogID)` | `{ bytes, files }` |
| `set(blogID, path, record)`, `setMany(blogID, [{ path, ...record }])` | Record paths, giving every ancestor without a record a directory record (S3 has implicit directories). Refuses a path below a file (ENOTDIR), a file over a directory (EISDIR) and a directory over a file (EEXIST) |
| `remove(blogID, path)` | Forget a file, or a directory and its whole subtree. Resolves to the removed record, or null |
| `removeAll(blogID)` | Forget the whole folder |
| `addMissingNames(blogID, { apply })` | Build the names set of every directory which lacks one (see above). Resolves to `{ dirs, missing, names }`; writes only with `apply`, and must run under the sync lock |
| `rebuild(blogID, records)` | Replace the index with records from a full listing (an iterable or async iterable). Resolves to `{ files, dirs, bytes, skipped }`. A record which conflicts with one before it (below a file, or a file and a directory at one path, which S3 can hold and a disk cannot) is skipped and listed in `skipped` as `{ path, type, code }` |

Removing a directory goes through its subtree deepest first, a page at a time, so each file's size is still in its parent's hash when the totals are corrected. If it stops part way, what is left is consistent apart from a lowercased lookup which may still name a child already gone, until its directory goes too.

---

## Files

| File | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `index.js` | The functions above |
| `key.js` | Key names, the path normalisation, and how a name is spelled in a names set |
| `record.js` | Packs and parses records |
| `tests/` | Specs, against real Redis |
